"01_view_overrides.mst — View overrides that must load AFTER st/mvp
 (DolphinDart DD10).

 LAYER ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING, for the third time in this project (the DD9
 alias globals and the depth-prefixed emission were the first two). These
 methods override ones the TRANSLATED `UI.View` defines, so a file in
 `st/dolphin_compat` — which loads before `st/mvp` — is simply overwritten by
 the class it is trying to adjust. This layer loads last:

   st/world;st/dolphin_compat;st/prims/rt;st/prims/structs;st/prims;
   st/prims/aliases;st/mvp;st/mvp_compat

 Nothing here reimplements Dolphin logic. Each method replaces a call into a
 part of Dolphin this port does not have, with the substrate's equivalent."

"── the class the door owns ─────────────────────────────────────────────"
nil subclass: View [
    "Dolphin registers a window class per View subclass and answers its name.
     Here every View is created against the DOOR's class, because its WndProc
     is the one that reflects messages into the image. Registering a second
     class with our WndProc would work identically and buy nothing."
    View class >> winClassName [ ^Win32 mvpTopClassName ]
    View class >> wndClassName [ ^Win32 mvpTopClassName ]

    "Dolphin asks the desktop for a creation parent when none is set, and then
     TALKS to it: `CreateWindowFunction>>scaleToDpiOf:` sends
     `scaleChildCreation:` to the parent. So the desktop cannot be nil — it
     has to be an object that answers the parent protocol."
    View class >> desktop [ ^DesktopView current ]
]

"── the desktop ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 The creation parent of a top-level window. Its HWND is 0 (CreateWindowExW
 takes 0 for `no parent`), and its DPI is 96 — the DD9 brief's recorded trim,
 `dpi ^96`, no per-monitor awareness in v1. `scaleChildCreation:` is therefore
 the identity: there is nothing to scale between 96 and 96, and inventing a
 factor here would move every window by a number nobody chose."
Object subclass: DesktopView [
    <classVars: Current>
    DesktopView class >> current [
        Current isNil ifTrue: [ Current := self basicNew ]. ^Current
    ]
    "THE REAL DESKTOP HWND, not 0. `View>>create` does

         creationParent isNil ifTrue: [self parentView: self class desktop]

     and `parentView:` ORs **WS_CHILD** into the creation style. A WS_CHILD
     window whose hWndParent is 0 is invalid, so CreateWindowExW answered 0 —
     with the same arguments succeeding when issued by hand, because by hand
     nothing had set WS_CHILD. Handing back the desktop window makes the pair
     consistent, which is what Dolphin's own DesktopView does."
    asParameter [ ^self handle ]
    handle [ ^User32 getDesktopWindow ]
    dpi [ ^96 ]
    scaleChildCreation: aCreateWindow [ ^aCreateWindow ]

    "`ShellView>>createWindow:` delegates the whole creation to its parent,
     because a container may want to adjust placement or styles first —
     `UI.ContainerView` has its own version. This is the DESKTOP's, and it is
     modelled on ContainerView's: settle the extent, place the window, hand
     the DPI down, then let the shell create itself.

     No DPI scaling: the desktop reports 96 and the DD9 brief's recorded trim
     keeps it there, so `extent * targetDpi // aCreateWindow dpi` is the
     identity and is written as such rather than computed."
    createShellWindow: aShellView withFunction: aCreateWindow [
        | extent |
        extent := aCreateWindow hasDefaultExtent
            ifTrue: [ self defaultExtentOf: aShellView ]
            ifFalse: [ aCreateWindow extent ].
        aCreateWindow
            position: (aShellView isInitiallyCentered
                        ifTrue: [ self centerExtent: extent ]
                        ifFalse: [ Point classVarZero ])
            extent: extent
            dpi: self dpi.
        aShellView dpi: self dpi.
        ^aShellView basicCreateWindow: aCreateWindow
    ]

    "A shell that asked for no particular size gets a reasonable one. Dolphin
     asks the monitor; 640x480 is this port's recorded default until
     per-monitor DPI lands."
    defaultExtentOf: aShellView [ ^640 @ 480 ]

    "Centred on the desktop's client area."
    centerExtent: anExtent [
        | r |
        r := RECTL new.
        User32 getClientRect: self handle lpRect: r address.
        ^(((r right - r left) - anExtent x) // 2) @
         (((r bottom - r top) - anExtent y) // 2)
    ]
    isOpen [ ^true ]
    parentView [ ^nil ]
    "`ShellView>>initialize` sets its parent to the desktop and then asks the
     parent whether it IS the desktop, to decide whether it is a top-level
     window. Answering true is the whole point of this object."
    isDesktop [ ^true ]
    view [ ^self ]

    "THE DESKTOP TERMINATES THE PARENT CHAIN. `View>>invalidateLayout` walks
     up via `parent childLayoutInvalidated`, and that method walks on
     UNCONDITIONALLY — `self parentView childLayoutInvalidated`, with no nil
     guard, because in Dolphin the chain always ends at a DesktopView that
     answers these. The desktop's own layout never changes, so they are
     genuine no-ops rather than stubs."
    childLayoutInvalidated [ ^self ]
    invalidateLayout [ ^self ]
    basicInvalidateLayout [ ^self ]
    onSubViewAdded: aView [ ^aView ]
    onSubViewRemoved: aView [ ^aView ]
]


"── running Dolphin's class-side initialize ─────────────────────────────
 Dolphin's package loader sends `initialize` to a class after filing it in,
 and six classes in this wave rely on it: `View class >> initialize` sets
 `NextId := 0`, builds the MessageMap and makes the ViewClosedError — without
 it `View>>defaultId` does `nil + 1`.

 The layer loader has no package concept, so this is the explicit equivalent.
 It is NOT automatic-on-load by design: the order matters (a class-side
 initialize may read another class's variables), and a list that is read and
 checked beats a traversal whose order nobody controls."
Object subclass: DolphinBoot [
    "A brace array: `Array with:` stops at four arguments in this dialect."
    DolphinBoot class >> classesToInitialize [
        ^{ Rectangle. Color. CommandDescription. View. ShellView. Menu.
           SystemMetrics. TextEdit }
    ]

    "Answers the classes that FAILED, so a caller can assert on the list rather
     than on a count. Each is attempted independently: a class whose
     `initialize` reaches something this port has not translated yet must not
     stop the ones that would have worked. Today that is `Color` (needs the
     colour-table classes) and `CommandDescription` (wants a weak lookup
     table) — both recorded here rather than quietly dropped from the list,
     because the list is the statement of what SHOULD initialize."
    DolphinBoot class >> initializeClasses [
        | failed |
        failed := OrderedCollection new.
        self classesToInitialize do: [ :each |
            (each respondsTo: #initialize)
                ifTrue: [
                    [ each initialize ]
                        on: Error
                        do: [ :e | failed add: each name ] ] ].
        ^failed asArray
    ]

    "The ones that must work for a View to be created at all. Each is
     attempted independently and the FAILURES are answered, for the same
     reason `initializeClasses` does it: a boot line that aborts half way
     leaves whatever came after it — `UiSession startUp`, in the gate that
     found this — silently not run."
    DolphinBoot class >> initializeViewClasses [
        | failed |
        failed := OrderedCollection new.
        { Rectangle. View. ShellView. SystemMetrics. TextEdit.
          CommandDescription. Menu. MenuItem } do: [ :each |
            [ each initialize ] on: Error do: [ :e | failed add: each name ] ].
        ^failed asArray
    ]
]

"── Utf16String ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 Dolphin's string class for UTF-16 text, which its loose `UserLibrary class >>
 getWindowText: hWnd` allocates with `newFixed:` before handing the buffer to
 the three-argument API call.

 This port's equivalent is `Utf16Buffer` (st/prims/rt) — same job, same
 layout, different name — so the name is ALIASED rather than a second class
 written. A global, because the builder resolves a capitalised name to a class
 first and there is no class of this name; the same mechanism DD9 used for the
 `User32`/`Gdi32` library aliases.

 `newFixed:` takes a CHARACTER count and allocates room for that many UTF-16
 units plus a null — the sizing `GetWindowTextW` expects when it is given
 `nMaxCount: len + 1`."
ExternalMemory subclass: Utf16Buffer [
    Utf16Buffer class >> newFixed: aCharCount [ ^self new: (aCharCount + 1) * 2 ]
]

Utf16String := Utf16Buffer.

"── CONTROL SUBCLASSING: the two places this door is not Dolphin's VM ────────

 A control is a window of a class COMCTL registered, so Dolphin gets into its
 message stream by SUBCLASSING it. `UI.ControlView` carries the whole
 mechanism already — `subclassWindow:`, `subclassWindow`,
 `defaultWindowProcessing:wParam:lParam:`, the `oldWndProc` ivar — and none of
 it is reimplemented here. What IS here is the two seams where Dolphin's VM
 does something this port's door does not, each named and no wider than it
 has to be.

 SEAM 1 — WHO CALLS `subclassWindow:`. Dolphin's VM calls it from a CBT hook
 during CreateWindowExW. This port binds views at WM_NCCREATE instead, in the
 door's own window procedure (`UiSession bindNewWindow:`) — which works for
 every window of OUR class and cannot work for a control, because a control's
 WM_NCCREATE goes to COMCTL's procedure and never reaches us at all. So the
 control attaches itself after creation, which is the same two steps in the
 same order: attach the handle, then substitute the procedure.

 SEAM 2 — THE DOOR NEEDS THE OLD PROCEDURE. Dolphin's VM reflects EVERY
 message into the image, so it never needs to know what to chain to. This
 door routes selectively (DD9 measured ~26us per routed message), and an
 unrouted message must still reach the control's own procedure or the control
 stops working. `Win32 mvpBindOldProc:proc:` hands the door the value
 `subclassWindow` has just captured — nothing is discovered twice, and the
 image stays the authority on what it subclassed. Proven by `st_subclass`
 before any of this was translated."
View subclass: ControlView [
    "SEAM 1. `super basicCreateWindow:` is Dolphin's, unchanged: it creates
     the window and answers with `handle` set. The subclassing then happens
     exactly where the VM would have done it — after the window exists and
     before anyone sends it anything."
    basicCreateWindow: aCreateWindow [
        super basicCreateWindow: aCreateWindow.
        handle isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        self subclassWindow: handle
    ]

    "SEAM 2. `super subclassWindow` is `View>>subclassWindow`, unchanged —
     it asks `VM getWndProc`, installs it, and records the answer in
     `oldWndProc` unless it was already ours."
    subclassWindow [
        super subclassWindow.
        oldWndProc isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        Win32 mvpBindOldProc: handle proc: oldWndProc
    ]
]

"`trimNulls` — Dolphin's spelling for reading a filled buffer back as a String.

 `Utf16Buffer>>stringValue` already does exactly this (st/prims/rt); the name
 differs because `asString`, Dolphin's other spelling, is a UNIVERSAL HELPER
 the IL builder rewrites at the call site, so a method of that name would
 never be reached. Dolphin's own `UserLibrary>>getWindowText:` and
 `getClassName:` both end in `^buf trimNulls`, so without this alias every
 text read out of a control answered `an ExternalMemory(2 bytes @ ...)`
 instead of the text — a wrong ANSWER, not an error."
ExternalMemory subclass: Utf16Buffer [
    trimNulls [ ^self stringValue ]

    "── and the TEXT protocol, because in Dolphin this IS a String ─────────
     `Utf16String` is a String subclass in Dolphin, so its own
     `UserLibrary class >> getWindowText:` ends `^text` and the caller treats
     the answer as text. Its comment says why it must stay UTF-16 rather than
     being decoded: other control APIs count in UTF-16 code units (the Edit
     control's EM_LINELENGTH).

     This port's `Utf16String` is an alias for `Utf16Buffer`, an
     ExternalMemory — right for marshalling, and NOT a String. So every read
     out of a control answered `an ExternalMemory(12 bytes @ ...)` where the
     code expected 'hello': a wrong ANSWER that flowed on into comparisons and
     presenters without ever raising.

     Giving it the text protocol closes that without decoding the buffer
     early. `printOn:` quotes, as a Smalltalk String does; `displayOn:` does
     not; `=` compares against a String or another buffer by content.

     NOT `size`: that is a universal helper the IL builder rewrites at the
     call site, and `ExternalMemory>>size` (the BYTE count) is what marshalling
     needs. Dolphin's `Utf16String>>size` is the CHARACTER count, so the two
     disagree — recorded here rather than papered over, because a caller
     wanting characters must ask `stringValue size`."
    printOn: aStream [
        aStream nextPut: $'.
        aStream nextPutAll: self stringValue.
        aStream nextPut: $'
    ]
    displayOn: aStream [ aStream nextPutAll: self stringValue ]
    = anObject [
        anObject isNil ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        (anObject isKindOf: Utf16Buffer)
            ifTrue: [ ^self stringValue = anObject stringValue ].
        ^self stringValue = anObject
    ]
    hash [ ^self stringValue hash ]
]

"THE FONT WALK'S TERMINATOR — scaffolding, with a named retirement.

 `View>>getActualFont` is `^font ifNil: [self parentView getActualFont]`: a
 view with no font of its own asks its parent, on up to the desktop. Dolphin's
 `UI.DesktopView` ends the walk with `^self font ifNil: [self iconTitleFont]`,
 a real `Graphics.Font` — and `Graphics.Font` is not translated yet (it lands
 with DD11's graphics wave).

 Rather than override Dolphin's font path — `ControlView>>applyFont` and
 `setFont:` are both on the create path and both would have to be stubbed —
 the walk ends at an object that satisfies the protocol they use:

     setFont: aFont
         scaledFont := aFont atDpi: self dpi.
         self sendMessage: WM_SETFONT wParam: scaledFont asParameter lParam: 0

 `atDpi:` answers itself (nothing to rescale) and `asParameter` answers 0.
 That is not a fudge: WM_SETFONT with a NULL font handle is Windows' own
 documented way of saying 'use the system font', which is exactly what an
 unstyled EDIT control uses anyway. So Dolphin's font code runs unchanged and
 does the right thing, and no method of Dolphin's is replaced.

 RETIREMENT: delete this class and the `getActualFont` below when
 `Graphics.Font` is translated (DD11). The gate that proves it is any test
 asserting a control's font is the one it was ASKED for rather than the
 system default — which is not assertable while this stand-in is here, and is
 the honest reason it is scaffolding rather than a design."
Object subclass: SystemFont [
    SystemFont class >> current [ ^self new ]
    atDpi: aDpi [ ^self ]
    asParameter [ ^0 ]
    isNil [ ^false ]
]

Object subclass: DesktopView [
    getActualFont [ ^SystemFont current ]
]
